<subsection> 
 <title>Formulas for multiple proportions and goodness-of-fit</title>
<p>
Difference of proportions (two samples):
</p>
<ul>
<li><m>n_1</m> and <m>n_2</m> are the sample sizes for the two groups.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p}_2 = \frac{\text{number of successes in group 1}}{n_1}</m> is the sample proportion for group 1.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p}_2 = \frac{\text{number of successes in group 2}}{n_2}</m> is the sample proportion for group 2.</li>
<li><m> \hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2</m> is the difference in sample proportions.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p} = \frac{\text{total number of successes}}{n_1 + n_2}</m> is the pooled sample proportion.</li>
<li><m>SD(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2)</m> is the standard deviation of the statistic.</li>
<li><m>\text{SE using pooled } \hat{p} =  \sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n_1}+\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n_2}}</m> is a theory-based standard error that approximates SD of the null distribution.</li>
<li><m>\text{SE using } \hat{p}_1 \text{ and } \hat{p}_2 = \sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}_1(1-\hat{p}_1)}{n_1}+\frac{\hat{p}_2(1-\hat{p}_2)}{n_2}} </m> is a theory-based standard error that approximates <m>SD(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2)</m>.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\text{standardized statistic} = \frac{\text{statistic} - \text{mean of the null distribution}}{\text{SD of the null distribution}} </m> is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.</li>
<ul><li><m> z =  \frac{\big(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2\big) - 0}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for two proportions.</li>
<li><m> z =\frac{ \big(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2\big) - 0 }{\text{SE using pooled } \hat{p}}  =  \frac{ \big(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2\big) - 0 }{\sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n_1}+\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n_2}}} </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for two proportions.</li></ul>
</ul>
<ul><li><m> z =  \frac{\big(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2\big) - 0}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for two proportions.</li>
<li><m> z =\frac{ \big(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2\big) - 0 }{\text{SE using pooled } \hat{p}}  =  \frac{ \big(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2\big) - 0 }{\sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n_1}+\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n_2}}} </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for two proportions.</li></ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\text{statistic} \pm \text{margin of error} =  \text{statistic} \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times \text{(SD of statistic)}</m> is a confidence interval.</li>
<ul><li><m> (\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2) \pm 2 \times SD(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2) </m> is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating <m> \pi_1 - \pi_2 </m>.</li>
<li><m>(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2) \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times (\text{SE using } \hat{p}_1 \text{ and } \hat{p}_2) </m> is a theory-based confidence interval estimating <m> \pi_1 - \pi_2 </m>, where the multiplier comes from the standard normal distribution.</li></ul>
</ul>
<ul><li><m> (\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2) \pm 2 \times SD(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2) </m> is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating <m> \pi_1 - \pi_2 </m>.</li>
<li><m>(\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2) \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times (\text{SE using } \hat{p}_1 \text{ and } \hat{p}_2) </m> is a theory-based confidence interval estimating <m> \pi_1 - \pi_2 </m>, where the multiplier comes from the standard normal distribution.</li></ul>
<p></p>
<p>
Mean Group Diff:
</p>
<ul>
<li><m>p_i</m>  are the population proportions for the groups.</li>
<li><m>n_i</m> are the sample sizes for the groups.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p}_i</m> are the sample proportions for the groups.</li>
<li><m>l</m> is the number of groups.</li>
<li>The Mean Group Diff is the mean of the absolute value of the differences of sample proportions, and can be computed directly as 
<m> \displaystyle \frac{\sum_{\text{groups } i,j} |\hat{p}_i - \hat{p}_j | }{ (l-1)(l/2) } </m>.
</li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<p>
Chi-square for proportions:
</p>
<ul>
<li><m>p_i</m>  are the population proportions for the groups.</li>
<li><m>n_i</m> are the sample sizes for the groups with <m>n</m> as the overall sample size.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p}_i</m> are the sample proportions for the groups with <m>\hat{p}</m> as the overall sample proportion.</li>
<li><m>l</m> is the number of groups.</li>
<li>Chi-square <m>\chi^2</m> is the sum of squared standardized proportions and can be calculated directly as <m> \displaystyle \chi^2 = \sum_{\text{groups } i} \left( \frac{\hat{p}_i - \hat{p} }{\sqrt{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p}) / n_i} }\right)^2 </m>.</li>
<li>The mean of a chi-square distribution is equal to the degrees of freedom <m> df = l-1 </m>.</li>
<li>The SD of a chi-square distribution is equal to <m> \sqrt{2 \ df }  </m>.</li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<p>
Mean Absolute Difference (MAD):
</p>
<ul>
<li><m> O </m>  are the observed counts for the cells.</li>
<li><m>E </m> are the expected counts for the cells.</li>
<ul><li>one-way table: <m>E = (\text{Expected probability } p_i) \times (\text{Total}) </m>.</li>
<li>two-way table: <m>E = (\text{Row total}) \times (\text{Column total}) / (\text{Grand total}) </m>.</li></ul>
<li>The Mean Absolute Difference (<m> MAD </m>) is the mean of the absolute value of the differences of cell counts, and can be computed directly as 
<m> \displaystyle \frac{ \sum_{\text{cells}} |O - E |  }{\# \text{Cells}} </m>.
</li>
</ul>
<ul><li>one-way table: <m>E = (\text{Expected probability } p_i) \times (\text{Total}) </m>.</li>
<li>two-way table: <m>E = (\text{Row total}) \times (\text{Column total}) / (\text{Grand total}) </m>.</li></ul>
<p></p>
<p>
Chi-square for tables:
</p>
<ul>
<li><m> O </m>  are the observed counts for the cells.</li>
<li><m>E </m> are the expected counts for the cells.</li>
<ul><li>one-way table: <m>E = (\text{Expected probability } p_i) \times (\text{Total}) </m>.</li>
<li>two-way table: <m>E = (\text{Row total}) \times (\text{Column total}) / (\text{Grand total}) </m>.</li></ul>
<li>The cell contributions are the values <m> \frac{(O - E)^2}{E} </m>.</li>
<li>Chi-square <m>\chi^2</m> is the sum of cell contributions and can be calculated directly as <m> \displaystyle \chi^2 = \sum_{\text{cells}} \left( \frac{(O - E)^2 }{E} \right)  </m>.</li>
<li>The mean of a chi-square distribution is equal to the degrees of freedom.</li>
<ul><li>one-way table: <m>df = \# \text{Groups} - 1 </m>.</li>
<li>two-way table: <m>df = (\# \text{Groups} - 1) \times (\# \text{Outcomes} - 1 ) </m>.</li></ul>
<li>The SD of a chi-square distribution is equal to <m> \sqrt{2 \ df }  </m>.</li>
</ul>
<ul><li>one-way table: <m>E = (\text{Expected probability } p_i) \times (\text{Total}) </m>.</li>
<li>two-way table: <m>E = (\text{Row total}) \times (\text{Column total}) / (\text{Grand total}) </m>.</li></ul>
<ul><li>one-way table: <m>df = \# \text{Groups} - 1 </m>.</li>
<li>two-way table: <m>df = (\# \text{Groups} - 1) \times (\# \text{Outcomes} - 1 ) </m>.</li></ul>
<p></p>
</subsection>

